Virtual machine tests

The tests in this directory boot a real machine. They are not part of the
validation gates, because they need QEMU with UEFI firmware, a kernel and
disk images.

Running

    tests/vm/run [--keep]

builds the static Laft binaries with four jobs and runs laft-vmtest, the
harness in src/. It needs qemu-system-x86_64 with the edk2 firmware in
/usr/share/qemu, x86_64-linux-musl-gcc, sfdisk, mkfs.vfat, mkfs.ext4 and
1.5 GiB of free memory, and uses KVM when /dev/kvm is usable. Nothing runs
as root. Downloads, builds, images and console logs live in
~/.cache/laft-vm, and --keep keeps the disk image after a successful run.

laft-build builds busybox, runit, the kernel and Limine in bootstrap mode
from the recipes in recipes/, which pin every source by URL and SHA-256.
The Laft binaries become the laft package, and laft-repo publishes all of
them as a signed file:// repository. An installer machine boots the kernel
directly, mounts the empty ext4 root and FAT32 EFI system partition of a
GPT disk image, and runs laft hardware --write and laft install with the
configuration in system.stav. The install runs in a machine because laft
install wants the EFI system partition mounted as FAT, which an
unprivileged user cannot do on the host. The disk then boots through the
firmware, Limine and laft-boot into runit, and the harness checks each
stage on the serial console, logs in as root, checks that every early
service succeeded, the boot state and the mounts of /laft and the
generation, and powers off with runit-init 0. A second boot checks that
the first one was recorded as good. A third boot, from a copy of the
disk, adds a service and changes the host name in the configuration,
runs laft apply, and checks without a reboot that the service runs and
the host name changed. The machine has no HPET, because with
UEFI firmware it would leave the CMOS clock without interrupts, which
hwclock waits for.

    tests/vm/run --self-built ~/.cache/laft-bootstrap/build/out

installs the base system that the bootstrap of the recipe repository
built instead, from the package files in the given directory: glibc,
dash, bash, coreutils, util-linux, shadow, udev, runit, dosfstools, the
kernel built from kernel.org source and Limine built from source, with the
configuration in system-self-built.stav. Only busybox, which the
installer runs, comes from recipes/. The self-built kernel boots the
installer too. The system boots without busybox, with udev populating
/dev, and the same checks run, together with one that fsck.vfat checked
the EFI system partition.

laft-boot

The unit tests of crates/laft-boot cover its decisions: the kernel command
line, laft-boot.json, mount options, superblock probing, device lookup in a
fake sysfs, generation selection, boot counting, the fallback with a power
loss after each of its steps, the boot menu rewrite, the order and flags of
the mount plans, and the root layout check. One test runs the generation
mount, the read-only /laft and the move of the kernel filesystems in a user
and mount namespace. What remains needs PID 1 on a real kernel and will be
tested here:

- Plain boot from an ext4 root without LUKS reaches a getty.
  /run/laft/system and /run/laft/booted point at mounts/<N>, and
  /proc/self/mountinfo shows /laft as ro,nosuid,nodev and
  /run/laft/mounts/<N> as ro,nodev without nosuid.
- Roots on xfs, btrfs and f2fs boot, and so does a separate /laft.
- A LUKS2 root boots after the passphrase is typed on the console. Two
  wrong passphrases followed by the right one boot, and three wrong ones
  drop to the emergency shell.
- For images made by each mkfs tool, UUID probing finds the UUID that the
  filesystem's own tools report.
- With the root disk detached, the shell appears after root_wait seconds
  and lists the block devices seen.
- A generation whose init fails is booted the configured number of times,
  then the machine falls back to the last good generation, in place when
  the kernel and initramfs are the same and through a rewritten
  default_entry and a reboot otherwise. In both cases default_entry names
  the last good generation afterwards, and a second reboot boots it
  again.
- Cutting the power while laft-boot falls back, before the boot menu is
  rewritten and again before boot.json is saved, still ends in the last
  good generation, with the failed one marked bad and one fallback event.
- laft.break=premount and laft.break=preswitch reach the shell, and
  leaving it continues the boot.
- fsck exit code 2 reboots, and 4 drops to the shell with the repair hint.
- A panic in laft-boot reaches the shell and then reboots.

Each test builds a disk image in a temporary directory, writes the ESP and
the root from a Laft generation, boots it with QEMU and OVMF, and drives
the serial console with scripts that wait for its output and type the
replies. The result is read from the console and from the image after
shutdown.

laft-enter

The unit tests of crates/laft-enter cover its decisions: the command line
and the view name rule, the limits on arguments and environment, the
owner, mode, type and size checks on view.json and privileged.json, their
format, name and version matching, the links /run/laft/system and
/laft/views/<name>/current, authorization by uid and gid, the order of the
privilege drop and its verification, the environment handed to the
command, the default shell, the path search, the working directory
fallback, the caller's umask for the command and the exit statuses 126
and 127. An integration test checks
that the binary refuses to run without the setuid bit. The mount sequence
is tested in crates/laft-sandbox inside a user namespace. What remains
needs a setuid root binary on a booted generation and will be tested here,
each as an unprivileged user unless stated otherwise:

- A user listed neither in a view's allowed uids nor through a group in
  its allowed gids is refused with exit status 1 and the message
  "laft-enter: uid <uid> may not enter view "<name>"". A user in a listed
  group is allowed, and so is root, who stays root inside.
- Inside a view, /usr shows the view, /etc/static shows the system's
  configuration, /proc/self/mountinfo shows the view ro,nosuid,nodev, and
  the host's mount table is unchanged afterwards. A USB drive mounted on
  the host while the session runs appears inside.
- In a view that inherits the system, doas true works. In a view that pins
  a different linux-pam, /usr/bin/doas is the view's own unprivileged file
  and doas fails without raising privileges.
- With /laft/views/dev/current pointing at a version other than the one
  privileged.json records for dev (arranged as root), entering prints the
  note that privileged programs are unavailable, binds none, and works.
- LD_PRELOAD=/tmp/evil.so laft-enter dev true never creates the marker
  file evil.so writes as root. MIMALLOC_ variables change nothing but the
  allocator's own diagnostics.
- Replacing /laft/views/dev/current with a symlink to a directory outside
  /laft/views, or view.json or privileged.json with a symlink, a FIFO, a
  file owned by another user or a group writable file (each done as root)
  makes laft-enter refuse with exit status 1.
- laft gc does not remove a view version while a laft-enter session for
  it runs, and removes it once the session and every process that
  inherited the lock have ended.
- A caller that closes descriptors 0 to 2, passes extra descriptors, sets
  RLIMIT_NOFILE to 3, ignores or blocks every signal, sets umask 0, or
  starts from a directory it cannot access either enters correctly or
  exits 1 with nothing mounted on the host. The extra descriptors are not
  open in the command, but the view.json lock descriptor is.
- Inside the session, /proc/self/status shows the caller's ids in every
  slot, no capabilities, and NoNewPrivs 0. Sending signals to laft-enter
  during setup never leaves a mount behind on the host.
- laft-enter dev missing-program exits 127, and laft-enter dev /etc/passwd
  exits 126.
- Nested use works: laft-enter other inside a session of dev mounts other
  and binds the system's privileged files.

laft

The integration tests of crates/laft run the binary with --root against a
temporary root, a file:// repository published with laft-repo and
packages built with laft-pkg: plan, apply, upgrade, switch, rollback, gc,
the generation, boot and package queries, /etc status and reset,
activation at boot, the configuration tools, the installer's checks and
the exit statuses. The worker protocol and the runit executor have unit
tests. What remains needs root on a booted generation and will be tested
here:

- laft apply on a running system starts the store worker. Afterwards
  /run/laft/system points at mounts/<N>, /proc/self/mountinfo shows
  /run/laft/mounts/<N> as ro,nodev without nosuid, /laft is still
  ro,nosuid,nodev in the initial namespace, and the previous generation
  is unmounted unless it is the booted one. Managed /etc symlinks follow
  and unmanaged files stay untouched.
- A service whose configuration changed is restarted, a new service is
  added to /run/runit/service and started, a removed one is stopped and
  removed. A service whose readiness check never succeeds makes laft
  apply exit 3, and the new generation is active and default.
- laft rollback restores the previous generation's packages, /etc and
  services, and the next boot uses it. laft switch <N> --boot only
  changes the default.
- An unmanaged /etc/resolv.conf that the new generation manages stops
  laft apply with status 6 before anything changes, and --adopt moves it
  aside.
- laft boot mark-good, run by the boot-complete service, and laft
  activate --boot, run by the laft-activate early service, write
  /laft/state through the worker while /laft is read-only.
- laft svc status shows up, pid and uptime for running services, laft
  svc start, stop and restart change them until the next apply, and laft
  svc log -f follows /var/log/<name>/current across a rotation.
- laft enter <view> -- <command> runs the command in the view through
  the setuid laft-enter.
- laft install --root /mnt onto freshly made filesystems with a FAT ESP
  at /mnt/boot boots, and laft rescue --root /mnt -- passwd root sets the
  password first.
- laft rescue and laft chroot from a rescue image enter an installed
  disk, laft rollback inside works offline, and after a reboot the
  previous generation is the default.
- laft doctor on a running system passes the laft mount and generation
  mount checks.

Early boot steps

The unit tests of crates/laft-svc run every built-in step of the early
boot graph against a temporary root with stand-in programs, and the
tests of crates/laft-activate render /etc/fstab. What needs root and a
real kernel will be tested here, on a booted generation:

- kernel-fs mounts devpts on /dev/pts, tmpfs on /dev/shm, cgroup2 on
  /sys/fs/cgroup and securityfs on /sys/kernel/security with the options
  its table lists, and efivarfs on /sys/firmware/efi/efivars under OVMF
  but not under legacy firmware. Running it again mounts nothing twice.
- kmod loads the modules of kernel.modules, so they appear in
  /proc/modules, and a blacklisted module listed by a package's
  /usr/lib/modules-load.d stays unloaded.
- udev-trigger leaves /dev/disk/by-uuid populated before mount-fs runs.
- hostname sets the name that uname -n prints to the configured one.
- mount-fs checks an ext4 /home with e2fsck and mounts it, mounts a vfat
  /boot, skips noauto filesystems, and leaves a filesystem whose check
  exits with status 4 unmounted while the others mount and the service
  is reported failed. At shutdown, mount-fs down unmounts them in reverse
  order.
- swap enables the swap lines of the rendered /etc/fstab, with the
  configured priority shown in /proc/swaps.
- random-seed creates /var/lib/laft/random-seed with mode 0600 on the
  first boot, and a different seed is there after every later boot and
  after a clean shutdown.
- sysctl applies kernel parameters, so /proc/sys/vm/swappiness shows the
  configured value.
- console loads the configured keymap and font on the virtual consoles.
- clock sets the system time from the hardware clock at boot and writes
  it back at shutdown.

Live switch recovery

The unit tests of crates/laft-store and crates/laft-activate interrupt a
live switch after every step and recover. What needs root on a booted
generation will be tested here:

- Killing laft apply with SIGKILL right after it mounted the new
  generation leaves /run/laft/system on the old one, and the next laft
  command unmounts the new generation and keeps the old default.
- Killing it after the switch of /run/laft/system makes the next laft
  command finish the views, /etc, accounts, services and unmount steps
  and make the new generation the default.
- Cutting the power at any of those points boots the old default, and
  laft activate --boot drops the interrupted switch and reconciles /etc
  with the booted generation.
